id: k8s.dns-service-discovery
kind: kubernetes
title: Cluster DNS and service discovery
aliases: kube-dns, coredns, service discovery
tags: networking, discovery, dns
questions:
  - how does one service find another in Kubernetes?
invariant: Every Service gets a DNS name `<service>.<namespace>.svc.cluster.local` resolving to its ClusterIP.
answer_30s: Cluster DNS resolves service names, so pods call `http://api.payments.svc.cluster.local` and never hardcode IPs.
answer_2m: |
  A Service name resolves to its ClusterIP (headless services resolve to Pod
  IPs instead). Search domains are injected into the Pod's resolv.conf, so
  `api` and `api.payments` usually work from the same namespace. The failure
  modes are the useful part: DNS resolution succeeding while the Service has no
  ready endpoints produces connection errors that look like DNS problems, and
  short names silently resolve across namespaces when a Service with the same
  name exists in the search path order.
when_to_use:
  - service-to-service addressing, config that must survive Pod replacement
commands:
  - kubectl get svc -n kube-system => is the DNS service itself healthy
  - kubectl exec -it deploy/api -- nslookup api.payments => what a pod actually resolves
failure_modes:
  - short-name collisions across namespaces
  - DNS fine but no ready endpoints (connection refused, looks like DNS)
  - CoreDNS under-resourced on a busy cluster, causing intermittent timeouts
related:
  - k8s.service
  - k8s.headless-service
sources:
  - https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/dns-pod-service/
